{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794391106,"nonce":"wDu+YVn/JTz8FKgXvGEvm3aPvCI=","title":"GPU animation P3: an idle frame loop for time-only effects, and wake scheduling","message":"Part of the GPU-deferral plan (umbrella issue). Depends on P2b. Closes the point of 59d424e.\n\nToday any animation keeps the pacer at vsync and every frame runs the whole path: event and state prelude, stylesheet state gate, CSS ticks, compositor fast path or repaint, and encoding, even when only a time uniform changed. `AnimationScheduler::step` also allocates a Vec of every registered spring each tick, and its cost scales with live springs, not active ones.\n\n**Wake scheduling.** The animation registry knows each descriptor's end time. While every running animation is GPU-evaluated, the app sleeps until the earliest wake (the next descriptor end, a caret toggle, a timer) and redraws only then. A frame that only advances the clock is a \"clock frame\": skip tick, rebuild and tree walk; re-encode the compositor blit and dynamic pass with the new time.\n\n**What it covers.** Flow shaders, spinners and skeleton shimmer (`@keyframes` on transform or opacity, through P2), ripples, caret blink (a CSS animation of the caret's opacity instead of a CPU wake), indeterminate progress. A gradient sweep needs a gradient-offset track (P5).\n\n**CSS additions (web standard).** `animation-play-state` (pause freezes the clock offset for that animation) and the `prefers-reduced-motion` media query (the clock policy jumps animations to their end). Neither exists today.\n\n**API.** CSS only. No Rust API change; `WindowConfig.animation_thread_mode` and the fps cap keep their meaning.\n\nAcceptance: an idle window with three spinners has CPU use close to an empty window (measured against the P0 baseline); pausing and resuming an animation is exact; reduced motion removes motion without breaking layout.","files":null}]}